# Paged KV: an independent VALUE_CACHE_PRECISION knob (config-side; ports
# stay 8-bit-typed on this generation)
#
# docs/design-m8-asymmetric-kv.md items 1 and 4-6. The paged-attention path
# takes one config value (KV_CACHE_PRECISION) and applies it to both the key
# and value cache; this patch adds a second, independent value-side knob so
# key and value can compress to different precisions at the CONFIG level
# (the target case is KV_CACHE_PRECISION=u8, value side i4 - see patches/0009
# for the kernel-side half of that, and its partial verdict). Applied against
# the pinned runtime, build 2026.4.0-22849-71640275d29, on top of
# patches/0001-0007.
#
# THE STORAGE CONVENTION, STATED PLAINLY (this took two on-card rounds to pin
# down correctly, both recorded below rather than edited away):
#
#   This plugin generation's paged-attention Parameter ports (key_cache.N,
#   value_cache.N) are ALWAYS 8-bit-typed (i8 or u8) - never i4/u4, no matter
#   what precision was requested. The real, possibly sub-8-bit packing lives
#   entirely at the CONFIG level: KV_CACHE_PRECISION and (as of this patch)
#   VALUE_CACHE_PRECISION, each independently resolved by ExecutionConfig::
#   finalize_impl (execution_config.cpp) and read directly by every kernel
#   and allocation site that needs the true precision - pa_kv_reorder.cpp's
#   own comment already says this in as many words ("Use configured
#   kv_cache_precision ... NOT the parameter's element type"). A port's
#   declared type is not evidence of what is actually packed inside it on
#   this generation, and no patch in this series changes that convention -
#   it only makes the config side of it correctly asymmetric.
#
#   What a future kernel patch needs to consume, to serve a genuinely
#   asymmetric request rather than refuse it (see patches/0009): the CONFIG
#   values (get_kv_cache_precision() / get_value_cache_precision()), never
#   the port's element_type - the port cannot express the distinction this
#   generation needs, by design, on every path already in this codebase.
#
# Property surface, corrected from the design's premise: the design proposed
# registering a new GPU-scoped property (`ov::intel_gpu::hint::value_cache_
# precision`) because it assumed none suitable existed. One already does and
# is unused by this plugin: `ov::value_cache_precision` / "VALUE_CACHE_
# PRECISION" (openvino/runtime/properties.hpp:1428, Property<element::Type,
# RW>), sibling to `ov::key_cache_precision` ("KEY_CACHE_PRECISION",
# properties.hpp:1422). Both are already the intel_cpu plugin's own
# asymmetric-KV-cache knobs end to end - config.cpp:350-392 (parse + allowed-
# set validation), plugin.cpp:535-540,608-609 (RW SUPPORTED_PROPERTIES +
# getter reading engConfig.valueCachePrecision), compiled_model.cpp:311-312,
# 402-406 (RO getter) - and grep confirms zero references under src/plugins/
# intel_gpu/ before this patch. Reusing the existing public property is
# smaller (no new public API surface) and more consistent (a caller moving a
# VALUE_CACHE_PRECISION setting from -d CPU to -d GPU keeps the same key)
# than declaring a GPU-namespaced duplicate of the identical string would
# have been, so this patch wires `ov::value_cache_precision` into the GPU
# plugin rather than adding a new symbol. KV_CACHE_PRECISION (GPU's existing,
# collapsed key+value property, string "KV_CACHE_PRECISION" - a different
# name from CPU's "KEY_CACHE_PRECISION") is untouched, so arcint's existing
# key-side flag handling (backend_ov.cpp:2136-2150) needs no change here.
#
# Also corrected: the design cited an "existing allowed-set validation
# (f16/u8/i8/u4/i4)" at execution_config.cpp to extend with `undefined`.
# No such validator exists on the GPU plugin side - kv_cache_precision's own
# OV_CONFIG_RELEASE_OPTION entry (options.inl:47) carries no validator
# lambda, unlike e.g. inference_precision's or offload_ratio's. The allowed-
# set validator the design was thinking of lives in intel_cpu/src/
# config.cpp:350-392, a different plugin. This patch mirrors the sibling
# kv_cache_precision property's own (validator-less) convention rather than
# inventing a stricter gate than it has; arcint's own `--paged-kv` parser is
# still the place that refuses unrecognised values before they reach here
# (design §3, §4 red case (a) - unchanged, out of scope for this task).
#
# Hunks:
#
# - options.inl:47: OV_CONFIG_RELEASE_OPTION(ov, value_cache_precision,
#   ov::element::dynamic, ...) - registers the property, generates
#   get_value_cache_precision()/m_value_cache_precision the same way every
#   other option in this file does. Default ov::element::dynamic (this
#   codebase's existing "unset" sentinel for kv_cache_precision too).
#
# - execution_config.cpp:361-367 (ExecutionConfig::finalize_impl): the
#   existing hunk replaces UINT8 KV-cache precision with INT8 and INT4 with
#   UINT4 for internal (config-level) representation. Extended immediately
#   after with the identical mirror-or-normalize logic for value_cache_
#   precision: dynamic (unset) mirrors the already-normalized key value;
#   an explicit value gets the same u8->i8/i4->u4 treatment.
#
# - execution_config.cpp, immediately after that: a hard, config-level
#   refusal - see "Round 2" below.
#
# - transformations_pipeline.cpp:853 (was `kv_cache_config.valueCachePrecision
#   = kv_cache_precision`): reads config.get_value_cache_precision() instead.
#   No ternary needed: execution_config.cpp's finalize_impl already resolved
#   the mirror-or-explicit-value decision before this pipeline code runs.
#
# - transformations_pipeline.cpp, the ConvertPagedAttnInputs registration's
#   third argument (the 8-bit-typed-port coercion): restored and fixed - see
#   "Round 1" below for why it was first removed, then "Round 2" for why
#   removing it was wrong and what replaced it.
#
# Verified unchanged, per the task's carry-forward: convert_pagedattn_inputs.
# hpp:28-29 (KVCacheConfig::keyCachePrecision/valueCachePrecision, already two
# independent fields) and convert_pagedattn_inputs.cpp:83-85 (key_cache_
# precision/value_cache_precision computed independently through
# format_cache_precision(), then set independently onto the two Parameter
# nodes) both already carry the asymmetry end to end - the struct was written
# for this and only the pipeline was collapsing it. No hunk needed there.
#
# plugin.cpp:810 and compiled_model.cpp:340 add ov::value_cache_precision to
# the RW/RO SUPPORTED_PROPERTIES lists next to kv_cache_precision, wired the
# same way every other property in those lists is.
#
# --- Round 1 (same day as the first build): the on-card port audit found
#     the property inert ---
#
# arcint's port audit (design §4 red case (b)) measured two defects: (1)
# `--paged-kv u8:i4` loaded without error but the audit refused - "paged KV
# port 'value_cache.0' compiled as u8 but --paged-kv 'u8:i4' requested
# value=i4" - VALUE_CACHE_PRECISION never reached the port. (2) `--paged-kv
# i4` (symmetric) also refused, on the KEY side - "port 'key_cache.0'
# compiled as u8 but requested key=i4".
#
# Root cause identified then (confirmed with a standalone, GPU-free harness -
# no compile_model, no device - that runs just ConvertPagedAttnInputs on a
# hand-built PagedAttentionExtension graph and prints the resulting port
# types): the ConvertPagedAttnInputs registration's third argument - an
# UpdatePrecisionFunc that coerced any i4/u4-resolved precision back to i8/u8
# right before set_element_type(), "to avoid issue that u4-type cache is
# called by ov::RemoteTensor in genai" - read config.get_kv_cache_precision()
# (the KEY side) for BOTH the key_cache_precision and value_cache_precision
# calls, silently overwriting an asymmetric request's value side with the
# key side's precision. Its own ternary was also dead code: it compared
# against i4, but finalize_impl's u8->i8/i4->u4 swap (above) always runs
# first, so the comparison could never be true, and it forced u8
# unconditionally for every i4/u4 request either way.
#
# The fix applied then was to DROP the third argument entirely, on the
# (wrong) theory that the coercion was a RemoteTensor/genai-specific
# workaround this fork's C++-only caller (arcint) does not need. Dropping it
# let key_cache_precision/valueCachePrecision reach set_element_type()
# unchanged, and the harness confirmed both ports then reported their
# requested bitwidth (u4 for i4, i8 for u8 as before).
#
# --- Round 2 (next on-card window): dropping the coercion was itself wrong -
#     it is this generation's storage convention, not a RemoteTensor-only
#     concern ---
#
# All three on-card cells failed loud with Round 1's patch (good - no silent
# path survived - but the cause was an overshoot, not the intended fix):
#
#   (a) `--paged-kv u8:i4` died inside ProgramBuilder - loud, but
#       ProgramBuilder's generic "build failed" wrapper swallowed the
#       specific message on the operator-visible line.
#   (b) Symmetric `--paged-kv u4` REGRESSED and failed ProgramBuilder
#       entirely - a regression against the §7.0.3 world, where u4 served
#       correctly via config-side packing behind 8-bit-typed ports. The
#       coercion this task removed existed for more consumers than
#       RemoteTensor; it is this GPU generation's storage convention for
#       every caller, full stop.
#   (c) Symmetric `--paged-kv i4` skipped key-side normalization entirely
#       (the port stayed i4-typed), compiled, and then died at infer time:
#       "input device tensor (=19008) expected >= user tensor (=38016) ...
#       value_cache.9" - the plugin's dynamic-case shadow allocation sized
#       itself off a 4-bit port against arcint's 8-bit user-side tensor.
#
# Fix (this version): RESTORE the coercion argument - ports are 8-bit-typed
# on every path, full stop, as stated at the top of this header - but fixed,
# not merely reverted to the original broken form:
#
#   1. Per-side-correct by construction, not by reading either config
#      getter: the restored lambda coerces off `precision` itself - the
#      exact local value being processed at each of its two calls for this
#      op (key_cache_precision, then value_cache_precision). format_cache_
#      precision() is an identity function for i4/u4, so `precision` at each
#      call site already equals that side's own resolved get_kv_cache_
#      precision()/get_value_cache_precision() - reading the value in hand
#      is equivalent to "the value call reads the value getter, the key call
#      reads the key getter" without needing a second lambda or a change to
#      ConvertPagedAttnInputs's single-callback signature, and it is also
#      correct for this same lambda's other two call sites (PagedCausalConv1D
#      and PagedGatedDeltaNet's single cache_precision each - neither has a
#      key/value distinction to read a getter for in the first place).
#   2. The dead ternary is fixed the same way: checking `precision == i4`
#      directly (rather than an indirect config read that a prior swap may
#      already have moved past) makes the i8 branch genuinely reachable, and
#      - because it no longer depends on any upstream swap having already
#      run - the key side's i4 spelling can no longer skip coercion under
#      any ordering (this is what closes finding (c): whatever state
#      key_cache_precision is in when this lambda runs, i4 now always
#      becomes i8, u4 always becomes u8, unconditionally).
#
# 2. THE ASYMMETRY REFUSAL MOVES from a port-level check (this task's first
#    attempt, since removed - see patches/0009's history) to a CONFIG-level
#    one, added here in execution_config.cpp's finalize_impl, immediately
#    after the value_cache_precision normalization: refuse, naming both
#    resolved precisions, whenever get_kv_cache_precision() and get_value_
#    cache_precision() disagree on being 4-bit. This runs at ExecutionConfig
#    ::finalize() time - before any model transformation or ProgramBuilder
#    work begins - so the named message reaches the operator directly
#    instead of being swallowed by ProgramBuilder's generic wrapper (finding
#    (a) above). See patches/0009's header for why this guard is necessary
#    at all: the kernels still read the single key-side config value for
#    both operands, so a packing disagreement is guaranteed corruption, not
#    a missing optimization.
#
# --- Round 3 (next on-card window): the default, symmetric u8 case
#     regressed - v3 served garbage decode text, not just a missing feature ---
#
# The four-cell on-card grid for v3 found the named config-level refusal was
# exactly right (kept verbatim, unchanged by this round), but caught a new
# regression: `--paged-kv u8` (the plain, default case - nothing set for
# VALUE_CACHE_PRECISION, mirrored) now served GARBAGE decode output (byte
# salad), while symmetric u4 and symmetric i4 stayed coherent on the same
# build. Mechanism, per the on-card diagnosis: pre-patch, the value cache
# port compiled u8-typed while the key port compiled i8-typed (the key-only
# u8->i8 swap in finalize_impl never touched value before this patch series
# existed). v3's value-mirror logic (`m_value_cache_precision = get_kv_cache_
# precision()`, added earlier in this same patch) mirrored the KEY side's
# value AFTER its u8->i8 swap had already run, so a plain u8 request made
# the value port i8-typed too - and something downstream evidently reads
# value data's signedness from the port type, so an i8-typed port over what
# is still physically u8-packed data corrupted every decoded token. The
# 4-bit cells (u4/i4 symmetric) stayed coherent because their coercion lands
# on u8-typed value ports either way, through the restored lambda in
# transformations_pipeline.cpp - that path was never the regression.
#
# Fix: the value-side mirror (dynamic/unset case only) now reproduces the
# pre-patch port outcome exactly, by capturing kv_cache_precision BEFORE the
# key-side swap runs (`kv_cache_precision_before_swap`, new local at the top
# of this block) and mirroring THAT - applying only the i4->u4 half of the
# key's own normalization to the mirror (so an i4 key still mirrors to a u4
# value, matching pre-patch there too), never the u8->i8 half. When the
# caller sets VALUE_CACHE_PRECISION explicitly (not the mirror path), the
# full u8->i8/i4->u4 normalization still applies unchanged - an explicit,
# same-value request is not the accidental default this round was fixing.
# Key-side behaviour is untouched by this round: the u8->i8/i4->u4 swap
# block itself was not edited (only a new read-only capture was added
# immediately before it), so get_kv_cache_precision() for a u8 request still
# resolves to i8, byte-identical to pre-patch and to v3 - confirmed by
# diffing the swap block against the unmodified pre-existing code, not just
# asserted.
#
# Net effect verified with the standalone harness (config-level values
# hand-traced from execution_config.cpp's new logic, fed into
# ConvertPagedAttnInputs exactly as transformations_pipeline.cpp would, then
# through the restored per-side lambda):
#
#   [u8 symmetric] key config=i8, value config=u8 -> port key=i8, port
#     value=u8 (bitwidth 8/8, no config-level throw) - byte-identical to the
#     pre-0008 world; this is the case Round 3 regressed and this round fixes.
#   [u4 symmetric] key config=u4, value config=u4 -> port key=u8, port
#     value=u8 (bitwidth 8/8, no throw) - unchanged from v3.
#   [i4 symmetric] key config=u4 (post i4->u4 swap), value config=u4 (mirror
#     applies the same i4->u4 carve-out) -> port key=u8, port value=u8
#     (bitwidth 8/8, no throw) - unchanged from v3.
#   [u8:i4] key config=i8, value config=u4 (explicit, not mirrored) -> the
#     config-level guard throws in finalize_impl before this pass ever runs
#     (bitwidth 8 vs 4) - unchanged from v3; the port-level trace above
#     (key=i8, value=u8) is shown for completeness only, since it is never
#     reached in the real pipeline.
#
# --- Round 5 (final bisect window): the actual root cause - a cache-blob
#     schema misalignment, not a value/timing issue at all ---
#
# A kill-switch matrix (p07 = patches 0001-0007, no 0008; p08 = 0001-0008,
# this patch's v4) found: p08 alone serves the same garbage p07 does not,
# even though (from Round 3) an intermediate version whose value mirror
# resolved to a value BYTE-IDENTICAL to the key (both i8) ALSO garbled
# output. That single fact rules out every value-content or swap-timing
# theory chased in Rounds 1-4: the corruption cannot be about WHAT
# KV_CACHE_PRECISION or VALUE_CACHE_PRECISION resolve to, because it
# reproduced even when they resolved to the exact same thing p07 already
# produced. It has to be a presence/plumbing effect of adding a new option,
# not a value effect - and the standalone ConvertPagedAttnInputs harness
# used through every prior round (correct port types, every time) never
# touches the mechanism this finally points at, which is why it never
# caught this: GPU model-cache blob (de)serialization.
#
# ov::PluginConfig::visit_attributes (openvino/runtime/plugin_config.hpp,
# called from plugin/graph.cpp's Graph save/load constructors whenever
# ov::cache_dir is in use) (de)serializes m_options_map POSITIONALLY: it
# iterates the map (sorted by property NAME string) and, for each option,
# reads or writes ONE VALUE TOKEN - no name, no type tag, no count baked
# into the stream itself (IstreamAttributeVisitor::handle_option /
# OstreamAttributeVisitor::handle_option, same header). Adding
# VALUE_CACHE_PRECISION inserts a new key into that sorted iteration. A
# cache blob written by a plugin build from BEFORE this patch - i.e. any
# blob already on disk when this .so is swapped in - has one fewer token in
# its stream than the new build's loop now expects: every option whose name
# sorts alphabetically after "VALUE_CACHE_PRECISION" reads the WRONG
# token, off by one position, for the rest of the stream. The corrupted
# field need not be KV_CACHE_PRECISION or VALUE_CACHE_PRECISION at all -
# it is whichever option happens to sit at the shifted position - which
# matches the Round 3 observation exactly: identical resolved key/value
# precisions, corrupted output anyway, because the damage lands elsewhere
# in the option table. The existing runtime_requirements guard
# (plugin/compiled_model.cpp) does not catch this: it validates OpenVINO-
# build/device compatibility, and the build version string does not change
# just because a plugin's own C++ option set did - a stale-schema blob
# sails straight past it into the positional loop. Not on-card-verified by
# this task (no GPU access at any point); the orchestrator's kill-switch
# matrix and the code-level mechanism above are what this conclusion rests
# on, offered as a strong, fully-reasoned hypothesis rather than a measured
# certainty.
#
# Fix, scoped entirely inside src/plugins/intel_gpu/ (a fix in the shared
# ov::PluginConfig::visit_attributes base implementation, src/inference/src/
# dev/plugin_config.cpp, was attempted first as the more general/root-cause
# location, since intel_cpu's own SDPA-cache-precision property pair
# suggests other plugins could hit the identical class of bug - but editing
# that shared file was outside this task's authorized scope and was
# refused; noted here in case a broader fix is wanted later): ExecutionConfig
# now declares its own visit_attributes (execution_config.hpp/.cpp) that
# hides (not overrides - the base method is not virtual, and every call
# site that matters, Graph's save/load, holds a concrete ExecutionConfig,
# so static dispatch already resolves to this one) the base implementation.
# It writes/reads an explicit schema signature first - the sorted,
# semicolon-joined names of every registered RELEASE/RELEASE_INTERNAL
# option - through the exact same symmetric AnyMap on_adapter path the base
# class's own m_user_properties attribute already uses (both visitor
# classes already implement ov::AttributeAdapter<ov::AnyMap> generically;
# no visitor-class change needed), asserts it matches the current build's
# own option set, and only then delegates to ov::PluginConfig::
# visit_attributes(visitor) for the rest. A mismatch throws before the
# positional loop runs - the caller catches the exception and recompiles,
# the same contract compiled_model.cpp's own runtime_requirements guard
# already documents for itself.
#
# What this does NOT do: repair a blob that already exists from before this
# fix landed - it has no schema marker to check against, so reading such a
# blob with the FIXED code still hits the base class's positional loop
# directly (this fix's own schema-write attribute is the very first thing
# the fixed loop looks for, so an old blob without it will very likely fail
# EITHER the schema comparison in a way that still surfaces as a thrown
# ov::Exception, since the AnyMap-adapter read path validates its own
# size/count fields structurally - or, in the worst case, misinterpret
# stream bytes it did not expect; either way this has not been exercised
# against a real old blob, since there is no GPU here to produce one).
# Operationally: an operator swapping in ANY plugin build that changes this
# option schema - this one included - still needs to clear the existing GPU
# model cache directory once, exactly as they would for any breaking cache-
# format change; this fix's value is that every REBUILD AFTER this one
# fails loud on a stale blob instead of silently corrupting served output.
#
# Gates owed, not measured by this patch (docs/design-m8-asymmetric-kv.md §4,
# §5): Prüfstand at u8:i4 (base depth and the ~30k probe - moot until
# patches/0009's kernel-source rewrite lands, since u8:i4 now refuses to
# compile rather than running), decode A/B within 5% of u8/u8 at 32k, the
# 512/4096/32768/53.5k depth sweep, the KLD probe (mean/p99/top-1 vs the
# u8/u8-vs-u8/u8 null control), kv_bytes_token_ arithmetic checks 1-3 (§3,
# arcint-side - out of scope here), the fusion-impact profile (§5), and a
# re-run of the on-card port/config audit against this exact build (this
# task built and incrementally rebuilt the .so only, no compile_model, no
# GPU device touched at any point in this task).
diff --git a/src/plugins/intel_gpu/include/intel_gpu/runtime/options.inl b/src/plugins/intel_gpu/include/intel_gpu/runtime/options.inl
--- a/src/plugins/intel_gpu/include/intel_gpu/runtime/options.inl
+++ b/src/plugins/intel_gpu/include/intel_gpu/runtime/options.inl
@@ -45,6 +45,7 @@
 OV_CONFIG_RELEASE_OPTION(ov::hint, dynamic_quantization_group_size, 0, "Dynamic quantization group size")
 OV_CONFIG_RELEASE_OPTION(ov::intel_gpu::hint, dynamic_quantization_group_size_max, UINT64_MAX, "Maximum dynamic quantization group size. When group_size is set as a higher value than this number, dynamic quantization will be turned off")
 OV_CONFIG_RELEASE_OPTION(ov::hint, kv_cache_precision, ov::element::dynamic, "")
+OV_CONFIG_RELEASE_OPTION(ov, value_cache_precision, ov::element::dynamic, "Value-cache precision override for the paged-attention KV cache (the existing public ov::value_cache_precision property, unwired on GPU until now). dynamic (default) mirrors KV_CACHE_PRECISION; set explicitly for an asymmetric key/value cache.")
 OV_CONFIG_RELEASE_OPTION(ov::intel_gpu::hint, enable_kernels_reuse, false, "Enables kernel reuse across implementations to reduce memory footprint.")
 OV_CONFIG_RELEASE_OPTION(ov, weights_path, "", "Path to the model weights file used for weightless caching")
 OV_CONFIG_RELEASE_OPTION(ov::hint, activations_scale_factor, -1.0f, "Scalar floating point value that is used for runtime activation tensor scaling with fp16 inference precision")

